    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "This study's cross-sectional design prevents determining causality. Additionally, self-reported data may introduce bias, with some responses possibly underreported or overreported. Although the EAT-26 and SMHAT-1 questionnaires are valid tools, their length (20-40 minutes to complete) made them impractical, potentially affecting participants' motivation and response accuracy. This likely contributed to the low participation rate, with only 110 out of 161 athletes completing the questionnaire. The lack of clinical diagnoses for high-risk participants limits the ability to confirm the accuracy of the results. Furthermore, the convenience sampling method may limit the generalizability of the findings. The exclusive focus on black belt athletes may limit the generalizability of the findings to lower-level or recreational Taekwondo practitioners, who may experience different levels of stress, competition, and training-related pressures."
